      {
        "review_id": 479519699,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r479519699-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "In the lap of Pashupatinath",
        "text": "The Pashupathinath temple was said to be built  by the Licchavi king Supushpadeva. Later in 1099 King shivadeva rebuilt the temple. Later in 17th Century king Bhupendra Malla again rebuilt the Pashupatinath Temple. \nAs per the early story Gouthama buddha performed a penance sitting in the middle of burning fire which is called as panchagni.Goddess Parvati was happy by this penance and advised Buddha to install a shivalinga on the banks of Manimati river. Buddha installed a shivalinga which was called as Karunikeshvara. \nLater the goddesses built a golden temple for Karunikeshvara and the place was called as Maheshvarapuri. As the centuries passed the temple was decayed and shivalinga was buried under the soil. Kamdhenu used to come to this place and anointing the shivalinga by its milk. The local cattle feeding people (pashupalaka) found this miracle and they saw the buried shivalinga. They started worshipping the shivalinga for the welfare of there cattles and their families. Then the shivalinga gained the name as Pashupathinatha- Protecter of cattle feeders.\nHere we can see a unique form of shivalinga that we cannot see any where. The linga is around 3-4 feet height. It is having Five faces  which are called as Sadyojata (Bhumi-Earth), Vamadeva (Water-Jala), Aghora (Fire-Agni) and Tatpurusha (Air- Vayu). The fifth place is called as Ishana (Sky- Space), this is facing above. \nWe can see Unmuktha Bhiarava temple, Vasuki nagaraja temple, Mukti mantapa, Santhaaneshavra Mahadeva temple in this complex. There are thousand shivalingas in this complex where we can see 525 shivalingas. Behind the temple Bhagamati river flows. There is a Ghat-Crematorium where every day the bodies get sacrament. \nBehind the temple there is a hill where we can see Guhyeshvari temple. Guhyeshvari is known as Maha shakti Peetha.   \nThe temple complex takes us to thousand of years back we can feel the journey in the Time Machine. I never forget the visit to Pashupatinath temple. The pashupathi nath temple is a place of nath cult. This is yet another big story.",
